WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:02.890
Leila runs a small hotel.

2
00:00:02.890 --> 00:00:06.767
A customer emails to ask what
the meeting room costs for a morning.

3
00:00:06.932 --> 00:00:10.821
Her AI helper replies in seconds,
polished and sure.

4
00:00:10.821 --> 00:00:14.042
There’s one problem.
It found last year’s price list.

5
00:00:14.322 --> 00:00:16.107
An AI can sound confident

6
00:00:16.107 --> 00:00:18.920
without using the right business record.

7
00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:22.435
A smooth sentence doesn’t tell you
where its facts came from.

8
00:00:22.715 --> 00:00:26.385
One way to help is called
retrieval-augmented generation.

9
00:00:26.385 --> 00:00:28.904
Think of it as an open-book answer.

10
00:00:29.069 --> 00:00:32.840
First, the app searches your records
for relevant passages.

11
00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:35.759
Then it puts those passages
beside the question.

12
00:00:35.759 --> 00:00:38.121
Then the model drafts an answer from them.

13
00:00:38.286 --> 00:00:41.336
The app supplies that context
with each question.

14
00:00:41.336 --> 00:00:43.849
The model isn’t retrained every time.

15
00:00:44.014 --> 00:00:48.880
In our fictional reception, the right
sheet slides into an evidence window.

16
00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:51.300
Leila checks that it’s the current
approved one

17
00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:53.198
before she quotes the customer.

18
00:00:53.478 --> 00:00:56.168
But an open book
can still be the wrong book.

19
00:00:56.168 --> 00:00:59.628
If search finds an old file,
misses an important note,

20
00:00:59.628 --> 00:01:01.708
or the index still holds an older copy,

21
00:01:01.708 --> 00:01:04.052
the answer can still be wrong.

22
00:01:04.217 --> 00:01:06.967
A source link helps you
inspect the evidence.

23
00:01:06.967 --> 00:01:09.627
It isn’t proof that
the sentence is correct.

24
00:01:09.627 --> 00:01:13.299
Open it, and check that it really
says what the answer says.

25
00:01:13.579 --> 00:01:16.899
So before you rely on an answer,
ask three things.

26
00:01:16.899 --> 00:01:19.920
Which record?
Which version, and what date?

27
00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:22.712
And does that record actually
support this answer?

28
00:01:22.992 --> 00:01:24.402
If the record is missing,

29
00:01:24.402 --> 00:01:27.882
the helper should say what’s missing,
not fill in a price.

30
00:01:28.048 --> 00:01:31.298
The useful change isn’t
a more confident sentence.

31
00:01:31.298 --> 00:01:34.697
It’s a traceable answer,
checked against the right record.

32
00:01:35.093 --> 00:01:36.563
Ahmadreza Samadi.

33
00:01:36.563 --> 00:01:38.500
